          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Nonstandard analysis is a rich formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            that uses a certain explicit notions of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+objects"} infinitesimal objects
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraproducts"} ultraproducts
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+set"} internal set
          %p
            In fact the nonstandard method is not limited to analysis, but is rather a method of producing a models in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+sets"} internal sets
            by Nelson.
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis+in+topology"} nonstandard analysis in topology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+set"} internal set
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p
            At its beginning, infinitesimal calculus was developed nonrigorously, though many interesting arguments and formal manipulations were found. Cauchy and Weierstraß introduced the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            approach, which enabled modern rigorous
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            = succeed "," do
              %strong nonstandard analysis
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Robinson"} Abraham Robinson
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilters"} ultrafilters
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+sets"} internal sets
            and using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            enable the foundation of nonstandard analysis. Many properties and theorems from classical analysis imply new statements of nonstandard analysis; the mechanism is the so-called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong transfer principle
          %h2#models Models
          %h3#an_approach_via_ultrafilters An approach via ultrafilters
          %p
            Assuming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            (whose full strength is not necessary), there exists a free (= not containing finite subsets)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} ultrafilter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}=\{1,2,3,\ldots\}
            = succeed "\u2013" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            correspondence with finitely additive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            (using the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} algebra of all subsets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}
          %p
            Fix a free ultrafilter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
            of real numbers,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a = (a_0, a_1, a_2, \cdots)
          %p of real numbers.
          %p
            We write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi F
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\sim_F g
            if the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{i\in\mathbb{N}|f(i)=g(i)\}
            belongs to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim_F
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^*\mathbb{R} := \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}/{\sim_F}
            is a
            %strong nonstandard extension
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %strong hyperreal numbers
          %p
            (This is a special case of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraproduct"} ultraproduct
            construction in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %p
            Given
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\in \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_F
            for its equivalence class in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^*\mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in \mathbb{R}
            the image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mi r
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi r
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^* r :=(i\mapsto r)_F
            of the constant sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  r = (r,r,r, \cdots )
                  \\,.
          %p
            is an element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{R}
            and this gives an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *:\mathbb{R}\hookrightarrow {}^*\mathbb{R}
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{R}
            is equipped with a strict total ordering given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi F
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f_F\lt g_F \;\Leftrightarrow\; \{i\in\mathbb{N}|f(i)\lt g(i)\}\in F,
          %p
            which makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *:\mathbb{R}\hookrightarrow {}^*\mathbb{R}
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotone+function"} monotone function
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta\in{}^*\mathbb{R}
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mo *
                      %mn 0
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^* 0\lt\delta
                is called a
                %strong positive number
                \.
            %li
              %p
                An element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
                such that for all positive
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in \mathbb{R}
                we have
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %mi r
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi δ
                      %mo &lt;
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -{}^* r \lt  \delta\lt{}^* r
                is called an
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong infinitesimal number
          %p
            Unlike in the real numbers, positive infinitesimal numbers exist: for example the class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_F
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:n\mapsto 1/n
            is such and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_F
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi n
                  %mo ↦
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:n\mapsto 1/n^2
            is a different one.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            be a nonnegative integer and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}
            a function. Then there is a nonstandard extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mi u
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^* u:({}^*\mathbb{R})^n\to{}^*\mathbb{R}
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi f
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi f
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ^*u(f^1_F,\ldots, f^n_F) = g \;\Leftrightarrow\; \{i\in\mathbb{N}| u(f^1(i),\ldots,f^n(i))=g(i) \}\in F.
          %p
            This is indeed an extension of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            in the sense that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^* u({}^*r_1,\ldots,{}^*r_n)={}^* r
            iff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u(r_1,\ldots,r_n)=r
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +,\cdot
            and the absolute value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\cdot|
            extend to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}*\mathbb{R}
            without putting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*
            in front, writing simply e.g.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi F
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_F+g_F
          %p
            To extend division appropriately, we need a little bit more care as it is originally just partially defined, so we need an extension of the formalism to subsets of the real line. In particular there is a following definition of an extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^* E\subset{}^*\mathbb{R}
            of a subset
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\subset\mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi E
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f_F\in{}^* E \;\Leftrightarrow\; \{i\in\mathbb{N}|f(i)\in E\}\in F.
          %p
            (For example, a number is positive, as defined earlier, if an only if it belongs to
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi r
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\{r|r \gt 0\}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℝ
                    %msup
                      %mo ×
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi r
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msup
                      %mo →
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{R} \times ^*\{r|r \ne 0\} \to ^*\mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/x
            is infinitesimal, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            itself is
            = succeed "." do
              %strong infinite
          %p
            Conversely, an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in{}^*\mathbb{R}
            is
            %strong finite
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |x|\lt {}^* r
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in\mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in{}^*\mathbb{R}
            is infinitely close to a unique real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\in\mathbb{R}
            in the sense that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x-{}^*q
            is infinitesimal. We say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            is the
            %strong standard part
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and is denoted by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %mi st
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q= st(x)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in\mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(r)
            of all elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in{}^*\mathbb{R}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi st
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} st(x)=r
            is said to be the
            %strong monad
            of the real number
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in\mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\subset\mathbb{R}
            is open iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(r)\subset{}^*E
            for all
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\in E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is closed iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi st
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} st(x)\in E
            for all finite
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in{}^* E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            iff, for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in{}^* E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is finite and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi st
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} st(x)\in E
          %p
            In this model of nonstandard analysis, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfer+principle"} transfer principle
            is a corollary of a general theorem on ultraproducts due Jerzy Łoś. It can be stated in terms of a certain formal language
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(\mathbb{R})
            of the real numbers. We can also extend this model to ultrapowers of larger sets, not just
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            itself, with a corresponding extension of the language. In the limit where we reach an entire “universe” of mathematics, this leads to the topos-theoretic filterquotient and sheaf models below.
          %h3#filterquotients_of_topoi Filterquotients of topoi
          %p
            The ultrapower construction above can be performed in the general context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            theory. From any topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            and any filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subterminal+objects"} subterminal objects
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}/\Phi
            %span.newWikiWord
              filterquotient
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/filterquotient"} ?
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+functor"} logical functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}/\Phi
          %p
            If
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo =
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E} = Set / \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            gives a filter of subterminals in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \to Set/\mathbb{N} \to (Set/\mathbb{N})/\Phi
          %p
            might be written
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*(-)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            is an ultrafilter, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/\mathbb{N})/\Phi
            is a two-valued topos, whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            is essentially that of the ultrafilter model described above. In particular, the global elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo *
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*\mathbb{R}
          %p
            In this context, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfer+principle"} transfer principle
            is the fact that the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^*(-)
            is both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+functor"} logical
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservative+functor"} conservative
          %h3#sheaves_on_the_topos_of_filters Sheaves on the topos of filters
          %p
            A different topos-theoretic construction is to consider the topos of sheaves on a category of filters. This topos models the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+set"} internal set
            theory of Nelson, a more axiomatic approach to nonstandard analysis. References:
          %ul
            %li
              I. Moerdijk,
              %em A model for intuitionistic non-standard arithmetic
            %li
              Erik Palmgren,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0168007296000413"} A sheaf-theoretic foundation for nonstandard analysis
            %li
              E. Palmgren,
              %em A constructive approach to nonstandard analysis
            %li
              E. Palmgern,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Remarks on a constructive sheaf model of nonstandard analysis
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1.1.30.4785"} doi
            %li
              Jonas Eliasson,
              %em Ultrapowers as sheaves on a category of ultrafilters
          %h2#measure_and_generalized_functions Measure and generalized functions
          %p
            The Lebesgue measure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}^n
            extends to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loeb+measure"} Loeb measure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi R
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^\ast\mathbf{R}^n
          %p The theory of generalized functions of Schwarz can be reproduced by nonstandard analysis:
          %p
            %strong Theorem.
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Robinson"}> Abraham Robinson
            ) Every generalized function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:\mathbf{R}\to\mathbf{R}
            can be represented as the integration of the product of test function with a nonstandard smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{f}: {}^\ast\mathbf{R}\to{}^\ast \mathbf{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mi d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (f,\phi) = \int \tilde{f} \phi d x
          %p
            There is also inutionistic version of nonstandard analysis approach to generalized functions as well as nonstandard approaches to Sato
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperfunction"} hyperfunction
          %h2#ToposModels Relationship to other types of infinitesimal
          %p
            There are other ways of realizing the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+number"} infinitesimal number
            precisely, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            and the
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surreal+number"} surreal number
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                While the most common infinitesimals appearing in SDG are
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent"} nilpotent
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/infinitesimal+number#NSAvsSDG"} here
            %li
              %p Since the surreal numbers are the universally embedding ordered field, any field of hyperreals can be embedded in the surreals. However, such embeddings don’t seem very useful, since they don’t preserve any of the important structure of the hyperreals (such as the transfer principle).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+nonstandard+analysis"} monad in nonstandard analysis
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"}> infinitesimal neighbourhood
              )
